During the ensilage process, giant reed harvested from October to December obtained lower (p
                        <0.05) losses of TS (0.6–1.1%), cellulose (2.8–3.6%), hemicellulose (4.3–8.5%), and lignin (5.0–5.6%) than giant reed harvested in August (Fig. 1
                        ), which was consistent with the higher acid concentrations and lower pH values that improved ensilage performance (Table 2). Fungal pretreatment resulted in higher TS losses (7.32–10.53%) than ensilage, especially for giant reed harvested after August (Fig.1a). The cellulose losses (8.30–11.86%) were also about double of those from ensilage (2.69–7.27%) (Fig.1c). However, the degradation of hemicellulose during fungal pretreatment was lower or comparable with that of ensilage (Fig.1d). WSC was the most easily degradable component in giant reed for both processes, with up to 95% of the WSC degraded during ensilage and up to 72% degraded during fungal pretreatment (Fig.1e). Extractives were also an easily degradable component in giant reed, with around 17.0–32.4% of extractives degraded during ensilage, and 19.7–34.1% degraded during fungal pretreatment (Fig.1f).
